# Axon Learning Rate

In this notebook you pick a learning rate for a small Axon model. Tune tries
three rates. For each one, your runner trains a fresh model on a fixed batch
and reports the validation loss. Tune keeps the rate with the lowest loss and
saves it as an artifact, while the trained model stays in your application.
The notebook runs on the CPU, offline, and needs no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"},
  {:nx, "~> 1.0"},
  {:axon, "~> 0.9"}
])
```

Use the pure Elixir backend so the notebook needs no compiled accelerator:

```elixir
Nx.default_backend(Nx.BinaryBackend)
```

## Prepare the data

The model learns `y = 2x`. Training uses one fixed batch of three points.
Validation uses two points the model does not train on.

```elixir
train_batches = [{Nx.tensor([[0.0], [1.0], [2.0]]), Nx.tensor([[0.0], [2.0], [4.0]])}]
validation = {Nx.tensor([[0.5], [1.5]]), Nx.tensor([[1.0], [3.0]])}
```

## Train inside the runner

The runner receives one configuration and one validation case. It builds a
fresh one-unit dense model, trains it for five epochs with SGD at the
configured learning rate, and measures mean squared error on the validation
points.

The runner returns the loss tensor and the trained model state. Tune treats
the output as an opaque value: only the score function reads it.

```elixir
fit = fn config, {features, expected} ->
  model =
    Axon.input("features", shape: {nil, 1})
    |> Axon.dense(1, kernel_initializer: :zeros, bias_initializer: :zeros)

  optimizer = Polaris.Optimizers.sgd(learning_rate: config.learning_rate)

  loop_state =
    model
    |> Axon.Loop.trainer(:mean_squared_error, optimizer, seed: 3, log: 0)
    |> Axon.Loop.run(train_batches, Axon.ModelState.empty(), epochs: 5)

  trained = loop_state.step_state.model_state
  predicted = Axon.predict(model, trained, features)
  loss = predicted |> Nx.subtract(expected) |> Nx.pow(2) |> Nx.mean()

  %{loss: loss, trained: trained}
end
```

Zero initializers and a fixed loop seed make every run repeatable.

## Search the learning rates

The target `%{learning_rate: 0.02}` is the original setting. `Tune.vary/2`
lists the rates to try, and grid search tries each one in order.

The score converts the loss tensor to an Elixir float with `Nx.to_number/1`.
Tune compares plain numbers, not tensors. `direction: :minimize` makes a lower
loss better.

```elixir
study =
  Tune.new(%{learning_rate: 0.02}, run: fit)
  |> Tune.data(validation: [validation], test: [validation])
  |> Tune.vary(learning_rate: [0.02, 0.1, 0.2])
  |> Tune.score(:loss, &Nx.to_number(&1.loss), direction: :minimize)
  |> Tune.search(:grid)

{:ok, result} = Tune.tune(study)

for candidate <- result.history do
  loss = candidate.evaluation.summaries.loss.mean
  IO.puts("learning_rate #{candidate.value.learning_rate}: loss #{Float.round(loss, 4)}")
end

IO.puts("Selected #{inspect(result.value)}")
```

```text
learning_rate 0.02: loss 1.7769
learning_rate 0.1: loss 0.0918
learning_rate 0.2: loss 0.0572
Selected %{learning_rate: 0.2}
```

With only five epochs, the smallest rate barely moves the weights. The largest
rate gets closest to `y = 2x`.

The validation split here is tiny and the search picks from it, so the loss is
optimistic. In real work, keep a separate test split that the search never
sees.

## Compare with the original rate

`Tune.compare/3` trains a model with the original rate and one with the
selected rate on the same split, then pairs their losses.

```elixir
{:ok, comparison} = Tune.compare(study, result, split: :test)
metric = comparison.metrics.loss

IO.puts("Original loss #{Float.round(metric.baseline_mean, 4)}")
IO.puts("Selected loss #{Float.round(metric.challenger_mean, 4)}")
IO.puts("Change #{Float.round(metric.delta, 4)} (#{metric.status})")
```

```text
Original loss 1.7769
Selected loss 0.0572
Change -1.7196 (improved)
```

For a minimized score, a negative change is an improvement.

## Save and reload the setting

`Tune.dump/1` builds an artifact from the result. The artifact holds only the
learned scalar setting. `Tune.load/2` applies it to a fresh target.

```elixir
{:ok, artifact} = Tune.dump(result)
{:ok, config} = Tune.load(artifact, %{learning_rate: 0.02})

IO.puts("Artifact parameters #{inspect(artifact.parameters)}")
IO.puts("Reloaded #{inspect(config)}")
```

```text
Artifact parameters %{"learning_rate" => 0.2}
Reloaded %{learning_rate: 0.2}
```

## Keep the model in the application

The trained weights stay in the observation output of the best candidate.
They are not part of the artifact.

```elixir
[observation] = result.best.evaluation.observations
IO.puts("Trained state #{inspect(observation.output.trained.__struct__)}")
IO.puts("Artifact keys #{inspect(Map.keys(artifact.parameters))}")
```

```text
Trained state Axon.ModelState
Artifact keys ["learning_rate"]
```

Tune refuses to put tensors or model state in artifact parameters. Store and
version the trained model with your application's own tooling. Use the
artifact to record which learning rate produced it, and train again with the
reloaded configuration when you need a fresh model.

